<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sibghatullah Mojaddedi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sibghatullah Mojaddedi was an Afghan religious scholar from a leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> family and leader of a moderate mujahideen party during the Soviet–Afghan War. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> Accord he became the first president of the Islamic State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in April 1992, serving for two months before handing over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanuddin-rabbani">Burhanuddin Rabbani</a>. He later chaired the loya jirga that adopted Afghanistan's 2004 constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sibghatullah-mojaddedi.jpg" alt="Sibghatullah Mojaddedi" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. State Department · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mojaddedi_in_September_2014_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
